Raw dev notes -- Taming RTL: Chat Composers and Stale Versions

This project, `smart-hebrew-rtl`, is my ongoing personal mission to make the web a more usable place for Hebrew speakers. It's a browser extension that injects custom CSS and sometimes a bit of JavaScript to fix the myriad of Right-to-Left (RTL) issues that many websites just don't bother to address. Think text alignment, cursor positioning, input direction – all the little things that make typing and reading Hebrew online a frustrating experience. It's a passion project born out of my own daily struggles.

This past week was a pretty focused sprint on some particularly stubborn UI elements. The big win was finally getting proper RTL support into chat composers and those tricky dialog option rows. These are places where users actively *type* or *select*, so getting the direction and alignment right isn't just cosmetic; it's fundamental to usability. It's not just about the text *displaying* correctly, but the entire interaction feeling natural. I pushed out `v2.2.5` with these fixes. On a slightly less glamorous but equally important note, I fixed a bug where the extension's popup UI was showing a stale version number. It was hardcoded, which was just dumb in hindsight. Now it reads directly from the manifest, so that's one less thing to manually update and potentially forget. Beyond the code, I spent some time outlining a more deterministic release pipeline and refining my strategy for monitoring selectors on target sites, because they *always* change. Oh, and I finally got around to cleaning up some old `CLAUDE.md` documentation that was just full of outdated version numbers.

Man, those chat composers were a real pain. It's never as simple as just slapping `direction: rtl;` on an element. You have to contend with `text-align`, `padding`, `margin`, and sometimes even `flexbox` or `grid` layouts that just fight you every step of the way. The cursor position was a particular nightmare; it would often jump to the wrong end of the line or get stuck. It felt like playing whack-a-mole with CSS properties, trying to override whatever the site was doing without breaking everything else. The stale version number in the popup was a classic "why did I do it that way?" moment – a good reminder to always centralize your source of truth and avoid redundant information. The selector monitoring plan is still a work in progress; sites are constantly updating their HTML, and my carefully crafted selectors break. It's a never-ending cat-and-mouse game.

Honestly, it feels incredibly satisfying to squash these little UI annoyances. It might seem like a small thing, but for people who use Hebrew daily, it makes a huge difference in their online experience. There's a real sense of accomplishment in making something *just work* the way it's supposed to, especially when it's something I personally benefit from. This work also highlighted how much I still need to learn about building robust, resilient systems – not just quick fixes. Thinking about long-term maintenance, stability, and a proper release process is where the real growth happens, rather than just hacking away. And the documentation cleanup, while tedious, was a painful but necessary reminder that stale docs are often worse than no docs at all.

Next up: Diving deeper into that deterministic release pipeline and making it a reality.
